[[:db/retract 17592186122378 :answer-field/choices 17592186122379] [:db/retract 17592186122378 :answer-field/choices 17592186122380] [:db/retract 17592186122378 :answer-field/choices 17592186122381] [:db/retract 17592186122378 :answer-field/choices 17592186122382] [:db/retract 17592186122378 :answer-field/choices 17592186122383] [:db/retract 17592186122768 :answer-field/choices 17592186122769] [:db/retract 17592186122768 :answer-field/choices 17592186122770] [:db/retract 17592186122768 :answer-field/choices 17592186122771] [:db/retract 17592186122768 :answer-field/choices 17592186122772] [:db/retract 17592186122768 :answer-field/choices 17592186122773] {:db/id 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" "question-5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" "question-3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" "question-beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-30b363a1-05a8-5997-8feb-6bd8aaa30fa0" "question-7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" "question-754f0abc-16b3-51ff-9446-aa81fd4c05d3" "question-72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/id #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36306" :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/id #uuid "cea3cee4-a045-5dc7-8596-6cf26a0cb161" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" "answer-4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" "answer-a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" "answer-eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" "answer-b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/id #uuid "d058285d-919c-56d4-bc37-2e175c0f050a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/id #uuid "4d2f5038-f42a-5fd7-a95d-a297da774c15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/id #uuid "a9d07651-85d5-5624-ae13-7a41e816c585" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/id #uuid "eb4ac023-e663-5e98-b2d3-aa3c2ec67064" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/id #uuid "b248e7e5-413b-5466-b7ed-293308db0e18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577" :question/id #uuid "5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577" :question/math-academy-id "q-130268" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ at the point $A(4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1⟩-⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(4,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(4,3) & =∇f(4,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-3)+3(-2)}{5\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be838696-b1cd-5c8f-81ac-b0d86172dd65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be838696-b1cd-5c8f-81ac-b0d86172dd65" :answer-field/id #uuid "be838696-b1cd-5c8f-81ac-b0d86172dd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" "answer-5d9b9f76-b123-5719-bc4e-29ca3f63e30e" "answer-b8aca910-6177-50b0-976d-f5a0cad4d3f7" "answer-502f0fe4-aa68-585a-bbd4-188d4c9372d3" "answer-455dead3-5a38-5f75-bb6f-3603611372d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer/id #uuid "021bf1f8-b881-536a-a8f1-f59bbd10c0cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9b9f76-b123-5719-bc4e-29ca3f63e30e" :answer/id #uuid "5d9b9f76-b123-5719-bc4e-29ca3f63e30e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8aca910-6177-50b0-976d-f5a0cad4d3f7" :answer/id #uuid "b8aca910-6177-50b0-976d-f5a0cad4d3f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-502f0fe4-aa68-585a-bbd4-188d4c9372d3" :answer/id #uuid "502f0fe4-aa68-585a-bbd4-188d4c9372d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-455dead3-5a38-5f75-bb6f-3603611372d3" :answer/id #uuid "455dead3-5a38-5f75-bb6f-3603611372d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124962 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=5i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{29} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}(5i+2j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =2\\,i+(2\\cdot 1)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2i+2j)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122372 :answer-field/correct 17592186122377 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124963 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2xy-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+{1}^{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122378 :answer-field/correct "answer-7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" "answer-476511f4-e638-543b-8280-a5b7db9ba896" "answer-b601e5c8-a43b-53e7-ba27-d8c6515bae90" "answer-5ebdb20c-42b9-5f29-a21e-e6adefd06cd3" "answer-6d736c41-9b37-5824-a068-b4c8dfbf00b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/id #uuid "7d2a5ab5-1e1e-50a2-abb5-df9207b0da19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\,i+j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-476511f4-e638-543b-8280-a5b7db9ba896" :answer/id #uuid "476511f4-e638-543b-8280-a5b7db9ba896" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-2)\\,i-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b601e5c8-a43b-53e7-ba27-d8c6515bae90" :answer/id #uuid "b601e5c8-a43b-53e7-ba27-d8c6515bae90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2i-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ebdb20c-42b9-5f29-a21e-e6adefd06cd3" :answer/id #uuid "5ebdb20c-42b9-5f29-a21e-e6adefd06cd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)\\,i+2j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d736c41-9b37-5824-a068-b4c8dfbf00b7" :answer/id #uuid "6d736c41-9b37-5824-a068-b4c8dfbf00b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2i+j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" :question/id #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" :question/math-academy-id "q-86539" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced" :answer-field/id #uuid "845bc3ce-d9e8-5c27-8b7e-c01d5d7a2ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" "answer-c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" "answer-01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" "answer-682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" "answer-604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" :answer/id #uuid "4cc668bd-9c56-5b86-a394-e8a7b8069688" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2e,\\,{e}^{4}-3,\\,12⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" :answer/id #uuid "c9528b5a-8771-5176-a89b-c59ec1b7b7ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2e-3,\\,-21⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" :answer/id #uuid "01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{2}+3,\\,12⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/id #uuid "682d87e6-5a4b-538f-8f34-c5cd1f0d46e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b" :answer/id #uuid "604a5840-27db-5147-b4e6-7928a771885b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{4}-3,\\,-12⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" :question/id #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83598" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" :answer-field/id #uuid "da37f824-4e73-5b4a-a18f-250ab74de323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" "answer-4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" "answer-b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" "answer-8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" "answer-8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" :answer/id #uuid "a78d148d-8a70-55a2-a873-68980ae2bd0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" :answer/id #uuid "4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-3,\\,-4,\\,4⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" :answer/id #uuid "b0d6b2ea-bb17-5bc1-89b4-f847433baa23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,\\,-2,\\,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/id #uuid "8b8bfd4d-1ee5-507f-b0d0-bf0d05bf14af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab" :answer/id #uuid "8e8a41f1-5149-587a-ba0e-8dfbff363aab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,\\,4,\\,-4⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40" :question/id #uuid "beaf6ecd-5ac6-5efb-ad1b-41d64d5bbd40" :question/math-academy-id "q-130273" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =⟨-6(1),-2(-\\frac{1}{2}),-3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-6,1,-3⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8901f1a6-9dd3-5894-ab03-0ab176fa8fea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8901f1a6-9dd3-5894-ab03-0ab176fa8fea" :answer-field/id #uuid "8901f1a6-9dd3-5894-ab03-0ab176fa8fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-314f45e4-cf29-50b1-9428-b22c82951ca6" "answer-f1d20d6e-c59b-5e2f-b608-e625a0868759" "answer-0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" "answer-4318aea0-1f2c-58b7-bdd1-6b19fbc76a15" "answer-46bb65c1-8132-59c6-9f4b-6909dff7bc8c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-314f45e4-cf29-50b1-9428-b22c82951ca6" :answer/id #uuid "314f45e4-cf29-50b1-9428-b22c82951ca6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-2,-5,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1d20d6e-c59b-5e2f-b608-e625a0868759" :answer/id #uuid "f1d20d6e-c59b-5e2f-b608-e625a0868759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-3,2,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/id #uuid "0e31501d-4531-5b76-9877-d2a3acbb5d11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-6,1,-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4318aea0-1f2c-58b7-bdd1-6b19fbc76a15" :answer/id #uuid "4318aea0-1f2c-58b7-bdd1-6b19fbc76a15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,2,-4⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46bb65c1-8132-59c6-9f4b-6909dff7bc8c" :answer/id #uuid "46bb65c1-8132-59c6-9f4b-6909dff7bc8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,-1,4⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30b363a1-05a8-5997-8feb-6bd8aaa30fa0" :question/id #uuid "30b363a1-05a8-5997-8feb-6bd8aaa30fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83609" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨3,-4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y(1+x){e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x(1+y){e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,-1) & =⟨(-1)\\cdot (1+1){e}^{1-1}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1),\\,1\\cdot (1-1){e}^{1-1}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33218d7b-e61f-5da1-bb1d-d60915785e82"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33218d7b-e61f-5da1-bb1d-d60915785e82" :answer-field/id #uuid "33218d7b-e61f-5da1-bb1d-d60915785e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e7ee258-aeb5-5bdf-92b6-350be2acf019" "answer-de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" "answer-d6b0abaf-e4dd-5cab-82a0-5a8354af1953" "answer-5b48cb68-dd75-5ec3-b835-523ae565ed53" "answer-2f930ebf-9d00-57ce-9436-e66479caf16c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e7ee258-aeb5-5bdf-92b6-350be2acf019" :answer/id #uuid "2e7ee258-aeb5-5bdf-92b6-350be2acf019" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer/id #uuid "de3f161a-4d79-5e42-a5c8-9b89385ad3f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6b0abaf-e4dd-5cab-82a0-5a8354af1953" :answer/id #uuid "d6b0abaf-e4dd-5cab-82a0-5a8354af1953" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b48cb68-dd75-5ec3-b835-523ae565ed53" :answer/id #uuid "5b48cb68-dd75-5ec3-b835-523ae565ed53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f930ebf-9d00-57ce-9436-e66479caf16c" :answer/id #uuid "2f930ebf-9d00-57ce-9436-e66479caf16c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" :question/id #uuid "7d39de67-7fd2-5aeb-a063-e9cf738816af" :question/math-academy-id "q-130806" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dc0783-a199-5ca8-ace2-ec2c865c730a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" "answer-a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" "answer-40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" "answer-7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" "answer-78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/id #uuid "7ced684c-4ebe-5a34-b02d-a8bda555a9f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" :answer/id #uuid "a3dd3ec0-5bd2-592f-a2b9-507da0e25254" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" :answer/id #uuid "40cd1b3b-3226-567f-8df7-b4203af0e8e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" :answer/id #uuid "7dfd9ca6-8542-5ff2-910b-fbe85fdf3f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c" :answer/id #uuid "78161357-66de-58ad-955b-760220e2284c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-754f0abc-16b3-51ff-9446-aa81fd4c05d3" :question/id #uuid "754f0abc-16b3-51ff-9446-aa81fd4c05d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-130804" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨5,12⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}=13 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x,\\,-6y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,1) & =⟨8(2),-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇g(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{16(5)-6(12)}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{80-72}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a49be9b-1ed8-506c-ad9a-611025859067"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a49be9b-1ed8-506c-ad9a-611025859067" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a49be9b-1ed8-506c-ad9a-611025859067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b5f201e-9679-590f-9849-011ab1fefcb6" "answer-86d2d586-ece9-5dd8-9965-84273626e130" "answer-60b225d0-997a-5fbc-a18b-ec3094252266" "answer-19005152-b76d-5f3e-8e83-f9fa12abf908" "answer-0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b5f201e-9679-590f-9849-011ab1fefcb6" :answer/id #uuid "3b5f201e-9679-590f-9849-011ab1fefcb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86d2d586-ece9-5dd8-9965-84273626e130" :answer/id #uuid "86d2d586-ece9-5dd8-9965-84273626e130" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60b225d0-997a-5fbc-a18b-ec3094252266" :answer/id #uuid "60b225d0-997a-5fbc-a18b-ec3094252266" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19005152-b76d-5f3e-8e83-f9fa12abf908" :answer/id #uuid "19005152-b76d-5f3e-8e83-f9fa12abf908" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer/id #uuid "0b6f9c9c-fd05-533b-ab92-794682300821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/id #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130811" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/id #uuid "716b4932-e73e-59f5-9ec5-4b4ed730dece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" "answer-c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" "answer-4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" "answer-94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" "answer-8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/id #uuid "c41e1274-b58c-591c-ba2b-30404efa267c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/id #uuid "c4350363-1f04-5bba-b31a-249136e6fc8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/id #uuid "4eb318ef-e5da-50bb-852d-abf0124a9413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/id #uuid "94e3a4fd-f234-5419-b576-fa4fcdfcf2eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/id #uuid "8d1128a0-3bc8-5ee6-8690-02b7875e3982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125028 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(e,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{x},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(e,\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}{e},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡e)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{e},\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\cdot (-1)}{e},\\,1\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(e,\\pi )$ in the direction of $∇g(e,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(e,\\pi )‖ & =‖⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{e},-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122768 :answer-field/correct "answer-5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" "answer-7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" "answer-5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" "answer-1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" "answer-cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" :answer/id #uuid "10d46f12-0b75-50c9-a911-0b663e2a7dd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi +e}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" :answer/id #uuid "7f7aff75-c59d-50b0-ace5-77c397f97e98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/id #uuid "5e6ee910-194a-5db1-a61b-cb7e82e92ef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" :answer/id #uuid "1683b5bd-9e3c-568d-b68d-d3cd665c9d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{4}+{e}^{2}}}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365" :answer/id #uuid "cfe40dc8-4b41-5a6b-bd2a-d1a558dc2365" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi +e}{e}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125736 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and a unit vector $\\mathbf{u} = \\left\\langle  u_1, u_2 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nThe **directional derivative of $f$** at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}$ is\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\dfrac{f(\\mathbf x_0 + t \\mathbf u) - f(\\mathbf x_0)}{t}.\n$$\n\nThe directional derivative of $f$ is the rate of change of the function $f$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$ We'll discuss this idea more later in the lesson.\n\nIt turns out that if $f$ is differentiable, we can write $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ in terms of partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf'_{\\mathbf u}(x_0,y_0) = \\nabla f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\mathbf u.\n$$\n\nAt the end of the lesson, we'll derive this formula. But for now, let's get some practice using it.\n\nFor example, let's find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)=(1,2)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{v} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(2\\cdot 1)\\,i+(2\\cdot 2)\\,j \\\\  & =2i+4j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,2) & =∇\\,f(1,2)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(2\\,i+4\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125737 :tutorial/content "From the formula for the directional derivative, we get that if  $z = f (x, y)$ is a differentiable function and $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector, then\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'_{\\mathbf u} &= \\nabla f \\cdot \\mathbf u \\[3pt]\n                      &= |\\nabla f| |\\mathbf u| \\cos\\theta\\[3pt]\n                      & = |\\nabla f| \\cos\\theta,\n}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the vectors $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/50/50c82908d5fd619c1c3c45f2aa3ff26929b8f369233c08d0688a091d2d3c123c.png)\n\n \n\nIt follows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\theta =0.$ In other words, $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is maximized when $\\mathbf u$ and $\\nabla f$ point in the same direction. Thus, the maximum value of $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ is $|\\nabla f|.$\n\nA similar argument shows that $f'_{\\mathbf u}$ reaches its minimum value $- |\\nabla f|$ when $\\theta = \\pi;$ that is, when $\\nabla f$ and $\\mathbf u$ point in opposite directions.\n\nAs a result, we get the following theorem:\n\n*A differentiable function $f$ increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction to that of its gradient.*\n\nFor functions of more than two variables, the same theorem holds." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125738 :tutorial/content "For a surface $z = f(x,y),$ the partial derivative $f_x(x_0,y_0)$ gives us the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{i} = \\langle 1,0\\rangle.$ But this is just a special case of the directional derivative, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{i}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{j}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & ={f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the directional derivative generalizes the notion of a partial derivative. \n\nNow, recall that the partial derivatives $f_x$ and $f_y$ represent the rate of change of $f$ in the direction of vectors $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j,$ respectively. As for a general directional derivative, we obtain the following property:\n\n*The directional derivative $f'_\\mathbf{u}(x_0,y_0)$ represents the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of $\\mathbf{u}.$*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125825 :tutorial/content "What is the directional derivative of the function $\\mathbf{f}$ at $(x_0,y_0)$ in the direction of the gradient? \n\nLet's apply our formula. First, we find the unit vector $\\mathbf{u}$ in the direction of $\\nabla\\! f\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}){‖}^{2}}{‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖} \\\\  & =‖∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude the following:\n\n*The directional derivative in the direction of the gradient equals the norm of the gradient. Furthermore, the norm of the gradient can be interpreted as the rate of change of the function in the direction of the gradient.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124730 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
